---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import AudioConverter from '@/components/tools/media/AudioConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="audio-converter">
  <AudioConverter client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert an audio file</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop in an MP3, WAV, OGG, M4A, FLAC, AAC, Opus or WMA file, or a video if you only want its soundtrack.</li>
      <li>Choose the output format. The estimated size updates immediately, so you can see the trade-off.</li>
      <li>Set a bitrate for lossy formats, a bit depth for WAV, or a compression level for FLAC.</li>
      <li>Open <strong>Advanced options</strong> to change the sample rate, force mono, or normalise loudness.</li>
      <li>Convert, listen to the result on the page, and download it.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Format reference</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Format</th>
            <th>Codec</th>
            <th>Lossy?</th>
            <th>Size for 3 minutes</th>
            <th>Choose it when</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>MP3</td>
            <td>LAME</td>
            <td>Yes</td>
            <td>4.3 MB at 192 kbps</td>
            <td>All devices must play it</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>M4A</td>
            <td>AAC</td>
            <td>Yes</td>
            <td>4.3 MB at 192 kbps</td>
            <td>Better quality than MP3 at the same bitrate</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>OGG</td>
            <td>Vorbis</td>
            <td>Yes</td>
            <td>4.3 MB at 192 kbps</td>
            <td>Patent-free pipelines, games, Linux tooling</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>FLAC</td>
            <td>FLAC</td>
            <td>No</td>
            <td>≈ 18 MB</td>
            <td>Archiving; about half the size of WAV</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>WAV</td>
            <td>PCM</td>
            <td>No</td>
            <td>31 MB at 16-bit/44.1 kHz</td>
            <td>A tool requires raw PCM input</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>The one rule of lossy conversion</h2>
    <p>
      Each lossy encode removes data that cannot come back. MP3 to FLAC restores nothing: it stores the
      damaged signal losslessly in a much bigger file. MP3 to MP3 at a higher bitrate makes the file
      bigger and adds a second round of damage.
    </p>
    <p>
      Keep your masters in FLAC or WAV, and make lossy copies from them. If you only have a lossy file
      and need another lossy format, convert once, directly, at a bitrate at least as high as the
      original.
    </p>

    <h2>Sample rate and channels</h2>
    <p>
      Sample rate sets the highest frequency the file can hold. 44.1 kHz covers human hearing up to
      about 22 kHz, 48 kHz is the video and broadcast standard, and 16 kHz is enough for clear speech.
      Downsampling a 48 kHz file to 44.1 kHz does no harm; upsampling adds only bytes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Forcing mono halves the data for a single-source recording, such as a lapel microphone or a phone
      voice memo, with no audible loss. Do not use it for music.
    </p>

    <h2>Loudness normalisation</h2>
    <p>
      The optional normalisation pass applies the FFmpeg <code>loudnorm</code> filter with a target of
      −16 LUFS integrated and a −1.5 dBTP ceiling. Most podcast platforms expect this level, and it
      fixes interviews where one speaker is much quieter than the other. It analyses and corrects in
      one pass, so it is a little less precise than a two-pass measurement, but well within the
      tolerance of those platforms. Leave it off for mastered music.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
